some people eat to live and some people  live to eat I am definitely the latter  in other words I have been a lifelong  foodie I'm sure most of you have fond  childhood memories you know a first  bicycle or a lemonade stand my fondest  memory takes me back to when I was 3  years old  recovering from the flu my mother had  forbidden me from eating mangos  but my grandmother snuck me one anyway  and I remember ecstatically running out  of the kitchen mango all over my face  and straight into my mother I told her I  hadn't eaten any mangos of course and  even she couldn't help but smile  reflecting on my childhood I am flooded  with memories like this memories  connecting food with a love so ingrained  in my upbringing that I could not  conceive of human suffering stemming  from hunger but this changed when I  visited my father's ancestral village  and met the children of some of his  childhood friends my peers enrolled in  schools just for the meal programs in  stark contrast walking through the  Dining Commons at Penn State barely a  few days later I saw trays full of food  being thrown away and I remember feeling  appalled at the amount of food that was  being wasted that was the moment I  realized that our food systems are  broken but it's not just the disparity  between what we don't have and what we  throw away we see ample evidence of our  broken food systems right here in our  own supermarkets I'm sure most of you  are used to seeing this but coming from  a culture where a milkman brought us our  one type of milk along with a cow to our  house every morning this dairy fridge  took me from odd confusion faster than a  Tesla can go from zero to 60 but this  fridge exists to cater to a wide array  of consumer  references because you and I don't make  food choices the same way you could be  thinking about health outcomes and  buying organic food you could be  thinking about environmental  sustainability and going local you could  be thinking about economic fairness and  going Fairtrade and the folks at the  back could be thinking about intangibles  like taste or color I like to think of  this as our food value hierarchy but  most of us share one fundamental value  in this hierarchy I'm sure you all know  what it is  price food retailers do as well so low  cost has been a predominant driver in  this industry for decades but as some of  the other values have started rising in  importance food retailers are extremely  slow to catch up why is that what is it  that needs to be fixed so I asked the  GSB community to tell me what they  thought needed to be fixed what was  broken with our food system and how they  would fix it wave a magic wand as it  were and then I went and spoke to  thought leaders and food advocates I  actually working to reform the food  system 80% of the GSB community that I  spoke to said that healthy food was  inaccessible unaffordable or  environmentally unsustainable the food  advocates agreed but I want to frame  this problem slightly differently how is  it possible that a vegetable that has  grown halfway across the world can be  produced harvested packaged marketed  transported and distributed at a price  point that is cheaper than a vegetable  that has grown sustainably just miles  from here the answer is simpler than you  think the food we eat is so cheap  because it has become a commodity grown  in a rigged economic system a system  that reallocates the cost of negative  impacts of production from the cash  register to our tax dollars and the  industrial complex the churns out this  food is big and it's made it so easy  first  prioritize cheap and unhealthy food  instead of demanding healthy and  sustainable food in fact the cards are  so highly stacked against resilient  farming systems that I fear that they  will never break through to become  mainstream unless we change our buying  behaviors and I hear you thinking this  is great Ruthie but I'm one consumer  against an entire system isn't that what  legislations for what can I possibly do  I hear you but I disagree because turns  out you can do a lot I have two  suggestions first recognize the cost of  industrial II produced food a pound of  conventional chicken cost about a dollar  fifty at the grocery store a pound of  premium chicken cost closer to two  dollars and seventy cents much more  expensive but as consumers we don't pay  the full price of that conventional  chicken because it's hidden in our tax  bill I've spent years thinking about  this problem and after wrestling with  data from the UN Environment Programme  the sustainable food trust the food tank  you name it I've come to realize that  when you add the cost of negative impact  so this could be the cost of runoff  clean up at factory farms incremental  health care costs associated with  antibiotic resistance in the country  that pound of conventional chicken gets  closer to two dollars and sixty cents  not that far off from premium chicken in  fact on average when you add these  negative externalities that's a 2 X  multiplier on the sticker price of any  conventionally produced food product  we're all familiar with NPV I like to  think of this as the future value of our  present choices and yes you don't pay  these hidden costs today but imagine a  future where you can't get cardiac  surgery because you have bacteria that  is resistant to all the antibiotics in  the market or a future where your child  is suffering from chronic asthma because  of environmental pollution and imagine  knowing then that your food choices in  the past had something to do with it so  think about that the next time you reach  for that industrial chicken off the  grocery  well maybe now you're thinking that the  price value that you place so much  emphasis on isn't quite right so  identify your other food values I'm  considering adding maybe just one more  to your hierarchy I like to think of  this as value training wheels  this could mean different things for  each 